{% set local_problem %}
<p>We pulled down gutters on a house off Airport Road last month that were supposedly "fine." The homeowner had been living with them for 12 years. When we got them down, every single seam was cracked and leaking — 7 joints total, all of them bad. The fascia behind the worst section was so rotted I could push my finger through it.</p>

<p>That's the problem with sectional gutters. Those joints are held together by caulk, crimped metal, or pop rivets — and Alabama weather beats them to death. Our summers hit 95+ degrees. Winter nights drop below freezing. That expansion and contraction works seams loose over time. Add the humidity that keeps everything damp, and joints start leaking years before the gutter material itself fails.</p>

<p>Seamless gutters solve this by eliminating the seams entirely. One continuous piece of aluminum from corner to corner — 47 feet if that's what your house needs, not five 10-foot sections caulked together. No joints to leak, no gaps to catch debris, no weak points to fail.</p>

<p>For Albertville homeowners who want gutters that just work, seamless is the way to go. Check out some of our <a href="/recent-projects/">recent seamless installations in Marshall County</a>.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are made from a continuous roll of aluminum coil stock, formed on-site using a portable roll-forming machine. We feed in flat aluminum, it comes out as a finished gutter profile — whatever length you need.</p>

<p>The machine lives in our truck. We pull up to your house, measure everything, then extrude each gutter run to exact length right in your driveway. A 52'8" run gets a 52'8" gutter, not a 50-footer with a splice.</p>

<p>The only joints in a seamless system are at inside corners, outside corners, and downspout outlets — maybe 4-6 total on an average house instead of 15-20 with sectional. Those few joints get hand-sealed with proper sealant (we use Geocel, not hardware store caulk) and will last the life of the gutters.</p>

<p>We use .032" aluminum minimum — that's 14% thicker than the .027" most big-box stores sell. The thicker material doesn't dent as easily, holds its shape better under ice loads, and lasts longer. On 6-inch gutters, we go to .032" or .040" depending on the application.</p>
{% endset %}

